---
/** Invoked Past — which yesterday the world cites today.
 *  Reads every dated reference in ~24 h of the world's press and publishes the histogram of
 *  invoked years. The published finding is the STANDOUT year, never the maximum: the maximum
 *  is the source's own 2014 ceiling and is labelled as the artefact it is.
 *  Data: src/data/invoked/latest.json for the day, every committed day file
 *  (src/data/invoked/YYYY-MM-DD.json) for the series, and register.json for the tracked events;
 *  git is the archive. */
import data from '@/data/invoked/latest.json'
import registerJson from '@/data/invoked/register.json'
import type { InvokedData } from '@/lib/invoked/types'
import {
  ageBucketLabel,
  count,
  decimal,
  humanDate,
  invokedByLabel,
  percent,
  standoutWindow,
  themeLabel,
  times,
  windowLabel,
  yearsBetween,
} from '@/lib/invoked/format'
import { shortDay } from '@/lib/archive/calendar'
import { standoutSeries } from '@/lib/invoked/series'
import { argumentHolds, decayArithmetic, lawReading, type InvokedRegister } from '@/lib/invoked/decay'
import InvokedPastFigure from './InvokedPastFigure.astro'
import InvokedSeriesFigure from './InvokedSeriesFigure.astro'
import SheetHead from '@/components/experiments/SheetHead.astro'
import DataAge from '@/components/experiments/DataAge.astro'
import SheetFoot from '@/components/experiments/SheetFoot.astro'
import '@/styles/experiment-sheet.css'

const d = data as unknown as InvokedData
const h = d.headline
const failed = d.stats.slots_fetched === 0
const neighbourhood = standoutWindow(d.method.standout)
const ceiling = d.stats.max_year_observed
const scope = `${d.method.year_window[0]}–${ceiling}`
// Usually the ramp toward the wall carries the raw maximum to the ceiling year itself, and
// the page says so. It is not a law, though — it is a nightly observation, and on
// 2026-08-16 it broke for the first time (1992 at 1,081 mentions over the wall's 882). The
// page therefore reads the file instead of asserting the habit: the wall is still measured
// and still disclosed, but whether the maximum sits on it is stated as what it is tonight.
const maxAtCeiling = d.most_invoked?.year === ceiling
const topYears = d.top_years.slice(0, 12)
// The evidence for the standout, published since method v1.1: themes, names and headlines
// out of the very rows the count came from. Optional, because day files written before
// v1.1 carry a headline without one — never because the page may skip the receipts.
const why = h?.why ?? null
const anniversary = why?.anniversary ?? null

// The series: every committed day file, read once at build time. A calendar day without a file
// and a day whose fetch failed are gaps in it, never bridged (src/lib/invoked/series.ts).
const archive = import.meta.glob('/src/data/invoked/2*.json', { eager: true, import: 'default' })
const series = standoutSeries(archive)
const todayYearDays = h ? series.days.filter((x) => x.standout?.year === h.year).length : 0
const recurringList = series.recurring
  .slice(0, 4)
  .map((r, i) => (i === 0 ? `${r.year} (${r.days} days)` : `${r.year} (${r.days})`))
  .join(', ')
const onceOnly = series.distinctYears - series.recurring.length

// The decay test that is not built, and the arithmetic that says why (src/lib/invoked/decay.ts).
const register = registerJson as InvokedRegister
const decay = decayArithmetic(register, archive)
const law = lawReading(d.law_test)
const eventCount = register.events.length
// "Every one of the 20 events was", "None of the 20 events was", "14 of the 20 events were"
const ofEvents = (k: number, singular: string, plural: string) =>
  k === eventCount
    ? `Every one of the ${eventCount} events ${singular}`
    : k === 0
      ? `None of the ${eventCount} events ${singular}`
      : `${k} of the ${eventCount} events ${k === 1 ? singular : plural}`
---

{/* The main landmark the skip link points at (#main), as on the other experiment pages. It also
    puts the article in block flow: as a direct child of the body's flex column, `mx-auto` sized
    the article to its widest figure, and on a phone the whole page scrolled sideways. */}
<main id="main" class="flex-1">
<article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-14">
  <SheetHead title="Invoked Past">
    <p class="mt-2 max-w-xl text-fg-muted">
      Which yesterday the world cites today. Every night the instrument reads every dated
      reference in the last 24 hours of the world's press and publishes the histogram of
      invoked years: which pasts were made present, by whose newsrooms, how old today's memory
      is — and, for the year that stands out, what the articles invoking it were about. Daily,
      archived, never edited.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 kicker">{d.date}</p>
    <DataAge generatedAt={d.generated_at} cadence="daily" />
  </SheetHead>

  {failed ? (
    <section class="mb-12 border-l-2 border-line pl-5">
      <p class="text-fg-muted">Source unreachable — finding omitted. A gap is recorded, never bridged.</p>
      {d.note && <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-fg-faint">{d.note}</p>}
    </section>
  ) : h ? (
    <section class="mb-12 border-l-2 border-accent/60 pl-5">
      <h2 class="kicker">
        Today's standout year — the one that most exceeds its own neighbourhood
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[clamp(20px,3.4vw,30px)] font-semibold leading-snug tabular-nums">{h.year}</p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-fg-muted">
        {count(h.mentions)} of today's dated references point at the year {h.year} —
        {' '}{times(h.times_its_neighbourhood)} the median of the {2 * neighbourhood} years around it
        ({decimal(h.neighbourhood_median, 0)} mentions), an excess of {decimal(h.surprise, 1)} times the square
        root of that baseline.
        {h.top_country_share !== null && h.invoked_by.length > 0 && (
          <>
            {' '}Of the mentions that could be traced to a source country, {percent(h.top_country_share)} came
            from {h.invoked_by[0].name}.
          </>
        )}
      </p>
      {h.invoked_by.length > 0 && (
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-fg-faint">
          Invoked by {invokedByLabel(h.invoked_by, 6)} · {decimal(h.per_1000_articles, 1)} mentions per 1,000 articles
        </p>
      )}
      {series.measured > 1 && (
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-fg-faint">
          Across the archive, {h.year} has been the standout on {todayYearDays} of {series.measured} measured days —
          {' '}<a href="#series" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">the series</a> says
          what one day cannot.
        </p>
      )}
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
        Why this year and not another is measurable, and it is measured directly below — from the same
        rows the count came from. And a year carried overwhelmingly by one country is as often an
        artefact of the extractor as a memory, which is why the country share stands beside the finding
        instead of behind it.
      </p>
    </section>
  ) : (
    <section class="mb-12 border-l-2 border-line pl-5">
      <p class="text-fg-muted">
        No year stood out from its neighbourhood above threshold today — a quiet day is a finding,
        and it is published as one.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  {!failed && h && why && (
    <section class="mb-12 border-l-2 border-accent/30 pl-5">
      <h2 class="kicker">
        Why this year — the evidence, out of the same rows
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-fg-muted">
        The {count(h.mentions)} mentions of {h.year} came from {count(why.articles)} of today's
        {' '}{count(d.stats.articles_scanned)} articles, and every one of those articles carried more than a
        date: GDELT's theme codes, the persons and organisations it extracted, the article's own headline.
        Those columns sit in the same rows as the count, so the reason this year stands out is measurable
        without leaving the file — and it is measured here rather than left to the reader to guess.
      </p>

      {anniversary && (
        <div class="mt-6">
          <h3 class="kicker">
            The anniversary, as arithmetic
          </h3>
          {anniversary.matches_today ? (
            <p class="mt-2 text-fg-muted">
              The exact date named most often inside {h.year} is {humanDate(anniversary.date)}
              {' '}({count(anniversary.mentions)} mentions), and the record's own date
              is {humanDate(anniversary.today)} — the same day of the year,
              {' '}{count(yearsBetween(anniversary.date, anniversary.today))} years apart. Today's standout is
              an anniversary, and that sentence is a comparison of two dates in this file, not a lookup in
              a calendar of events.
            </p>
          ) : (
            <p class="mt-2 text-fg-muted">
              The exact date named most often inside {h.year} is {humanDate(anniversary.date)}
              {' '}({count(anniversary.mentions)} mentions) — not the record's own day of the year
              ({humanDate(anniversary.today)}). By this instrument's arithmetic the standout is
              <em>not</em> an anniversary today, so it is not called one; what it is instead stands in the
              themes and the headlines below.
            </p>
          )}
          {why.top_exact_dates.length > 0 && (
            <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs text-fg-faint">
              Exact dates named inside {h.year}: {why.top_exact_dates.map((e, i) => (
                <>{i > 0 ? ' · ' : ''}{humanDate(e.date)} ({count(e.mentions)})</>
              ))}
            </p>
          )}
        </div>
      )}

      {why.themes.length > 0 && (
        <div class="mt-6">
          <h3 class="kicker">
            What the invoking articles were about
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">
            GDELT's own theme codes, ranked by <strong>lift</strong>: how much more common a code is among the
            {' '}{count(why.articles)} invoking articles than across the day's articles as a whole. Ranked by
            raw frequency instead, the list returns the codes that sit on every day of the year and says
            nothing about this one. The raw code stands beside every label — the label is a reading, the code
            is the checkable thing.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm tabular-nums">
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-line text-left kicker">
                  <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium">Theme</th>
                  <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-right font-medium">articles</th>
                  <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-right font-medium" title="share of the articles that invoked the standout year">share</th>
                  <th class="py-2 text-right font-medium" title="times more common here than across the whole day">lift</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {why.themes.map((t) => (
                  <tr class="border-b border-line/50">
                    <td class="py-1.5 pr-3">
                      <span title={t.code}>{themeLabel(t.code)}</span>
                      <span class="ml-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-faint">{t.code}</span>
                    </td>
                    <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 text-right font-mono text-xs">{count(t.articles)}</td>
                    <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 text-right font-mono text-xs">{percent(t.share)}</td>
                    <td class="py-1.5 text-right font-mono text-xs">{times(t.lift)}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-faint">
            Three floors before a theme is listed: at least 15 invoking articles, at least 8% of them, and a
            lift of at least 2 — lift on its own crowns rarity, and rarity is not relevance.
          </p>
        </div>
      )}

      {(why.persons.length > 0 || why.organisations.length > 0) && (
        <div class="mt-6">
          <h3 class="kicker">
            The names in those articles
          </h3>
          <div class="mt-3 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
            {why.persons.length > 0 && (
              <div>
                <p class="kicker">Persons</p>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm">
                  {why.persons.map((p) => (
                    <li class="flex justify-between gap-3 border-b border-line/50 pb-1">
                      <span>{p.name}</span>
                      <span class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-fg-faint">{count(p.articles)}</span>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>
            )}
            {why.organisations.length > 0 && (
              <div>
                <p class="kicker">Organisations</p>
                <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm">
                  {why.organisations.map((o) => (
                    <li class="flex justify-between gap-3 border-b border-line/50 pb-1">
                      <span>{o.name}</span>
                      <span class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-fg-faint">{count(o.articles)}</span>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>
            )}
          </div>
          <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-faint">
            Counted in invoking articles, and printed exactly as the extractor listed them — lowercase, and
            regularly holding phrases that are neither a person nor an organisation. Tidying that list would
            be this instrument's opinion about what belongs in it.
          </p>
        </div>
      )}

      {why.headlines.length > 0 && (
        <div class="mt-6">
          <h3 class="kicker">
            The articles themselves
          </h3>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3">
            {why.headlines.map((a) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={a.url}
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener nofollow"
                  class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg"
                >{a.title}</a>
                <span class="ml-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-faint">{a.domain}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-faint">
            Up to five of the invoking articles, those naming a full date inside {h.year} first. Titles and
            links as the source recorded them — quoted, not summarised.
          </p>
        </div>
      )}

      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-fg-muted">
        One line does not move: the instrument never names the event. It publishes what the press wrote and
        leaves the naming to whoever reads it — showing the evidence is a measurement, supplying the answer
        would be an oracle.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  {!failed && d.years.length > 0 && (
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="kicker">
        The day's memory, year by year
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-4">
        <InvokedPastFigure data={d} />
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  {series.measured > 0 && (
    <section id="series" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="kicker">
        The standout, day by day — {series.days.length} days of the archive
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-fg-muted">
        A single day names the year that breaks the ramp; only the archive can say whether that year breaks it again.
        Since {humanDate(series.first ?? d.date)}, {series.withStandout} of {series.measured} measured days had a
        standout{series.quiet > 0 ? ` and ${series.quiet} were quiet` : ''}{series.failed + series.missing > 0
          ? `, and ${series.failed + series.missing} calendar days carry no measurement at all`
          : ''}. Between them they named {series.distinctYears} different years, {onceOnly} of them exactly
        once{series.recurring.length > 0 ? `; ${series.recurring.length} stood out on more than one day, led by ${recurringList}` : ''}.
        {series.longestRun && (
          <>
            {' '}The longest run is {series.longestRun.year}: {series.longestRun.days} consecutive days, from
            {' '}{shortDay(series.longestRun.from)} to {shortDay(series.longestRun.to)}.
          </>
        )}
        {series.anniversaries > 0 && (
          <>
            {' '}On {`${series.anniversaries} ${series.anniversaries === 1 ? 'day' : 'days'}`} the standout's most-named
            exact date fell on the record's own calendar day — an anniversary by the instrument's arithmetic, and ringed
            in the figure.
          </>
        )}
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4">
        <InvokedSeriesFigure series={series} />
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  {!failed && d.most_invoked && (
    <section class="mb-12 border-l-2 border-line pl-5">
      <h2 class="kicker">
        {maxAtCeiling
          ? 'The most-invoked year is an artefact, not the finding'
          : 'Tonight the most-invoked year is not the wall'}
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-fg-muted">
        Today's raw maximum is <strong>{d.most_invoked.year}</strong> with {count(d.most_invoked.mentions)} mentions
        ({decimal(d.most_invoked.per_1000_articles, 1)} per 1,000 articles). GDELT's date extractor emits no year
        later than {ceiling}: measured as a clean cliff when this instrument was built, and re-measured every
        night as the highest year the day's files contain (tonight: {ceiling}). Attention decays with age, so the
        ramp toward the newest available year is the law rather than the news.
        {maxAtCeiling ? (
          <>
            {' '}The maximum sits on that wall tonight — as it did on {series.maximumAtCeiling} of the
            {' '}{series.measured} measured days in the archive — and it is published here as an artefact of the source
            rather than as a finding about the past.
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            {' '}Tonight the maximum does <strong>not</strong> sit on that wall: {d.most_invoked.year} outranks
            the ceiling year {ceiling}, which the ramp alone does not account for (across the archive the maximum sat
            on the wall on {series.maximumAtCeiling} of {series.measured} measured days). The wall is unchanged and
            still drawn as the empty band it is; what changed is that a year below it drew more invocation than
            the newest year the source will emit. Whether that is a story about {d.most_invoked.year} or about a
            thin day at the ceiling is not settled by this count, and the page does not settle it.
          </>
        )}
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
        The scope of this instrument is therefore the press's memory of <strong>{scope}</strong>. Invocation of the
        last decade is <em>not available</em> from this field, and it is not substituted from anywhere else: a
        title-level extraction would be a different population, and blending populations is how instruments start
        lying. The wall is inherited, disclosed, and drawn into the figure as the empty band it is.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  {!failed && d.age_profile.length > 0 && (
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="kicker">
        How old is today's memory
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm tabular-nums">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-left kicker">
              <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium">Age of the invoked year</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-right font-medium">mentions</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-right font-medium">share of the day</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {d.age_profile.map((b) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-line/50">
                <td class="py-1.5 pr-3">{ageBucketLabel(b)}</td>
                <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 text-right font-mono text-xs">{count(b.mentions)}</td>
                <td class="py-1.5 text-right font-mono text-xs">{percent(b.share)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      {d.age_profile[0] && d.age_profile[0].mentions === 0 && (
        <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-faint">
          The youngest bucket is empty because of the {ceiling} wall, not because the press stopped citing the
          last decade: nothing after {ceiling} reaches this instrument at all.
        </p>
      )}
    </section>
  )}

  {!failed && topYears.length > 0 && (
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="kicker">
        Today's most invoked years — a count, not a finding
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm tabular-nums">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-left kicker">
              <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium">Year</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-right font-medium">mentions</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-right font-medium" title="mentions per 1,000 articles scanned">per 1,000</th>
              <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-right font-medium">share</th>
              <th class="py-2 font-medium">invoked by</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {topYears.map((t) => (
              <tr class="border-b border-line/50">
                <td class:list={['py-1.5 pr-3', h && t.year === h.year ? 'font-semibold' : '']}>
                  {t.year}{h && t.year === h.year ? ' — the standout' : ''}{t.year === ceiling ? ' — at the wall' : ''}
                </td>
                <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 text-right font-mono text-xs">{count(t.mentions)}</td>
                <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 text-right font-mono text-xs">{decimal(t.per_1000_articles, 1)}</td>
                <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 text-right font-mono text-xs">{percent(t.share)}</td>
                <td class="py-1.5 font-mono text-xs text-fg-faint">{invokedByLabel(t.invoked_by, 3)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-faint">
        Ranked by today's mentions; the newest years sit highest because the source's ceiling ends there, which
        is why this table is a count and not a finding. “Invoked by” names the three largest source countries of
        that year's mentions — source country, not subject: it says whose newsrooms wrote, never whose past it is.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="mb-12">
    <h2 class="kicker">
      The decay law: not built — and why this archive cannot build it
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-fg-muted">
      The test hypothesis of this instrument was the biexponential decay of collective memory —
      <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-018-0474-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">Candia et&nbsp;al. 2019</a>:
      attention to an item falls in two phases, a fast communicative one and a slow cultural one. The plan was to
      fit that curve to the daily mentions of the tracked events, a register of {eventCount} exact dates founded on
      {' '}{humanDate(register.founded)} from the archive's first {decay.foundingDays} days. That fit is not built,
      and this page no longer promises it.
    </p>
    {argumentHolds(decay) ? (
      <p class="mt-3 text-fg-muted">
        <strong>The clock is wrong by a factor of years.</strong> Candia's curve describes attention as a function of an
        item's age, measured in years: in the paper's own data, communicative memory lasts about five and a half years
        for songs and two to three decades for biographies. The tracked events are {decay.minAge} to {decay.maxAge} years
        old, and the {decay.spanDays} days since the register was founded have moved each of them by
        {' '}{decimal(decay.ageShiftYears, 2)} of a year. Over a stretch that short the law predicts a line that is
        practically flat; what the series records instead is the news.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p class="mt-3 text-fg-muted">
        <strong>This part of the case is due to be re-read.</strong> It argued that the series was far too short to
        move the events' ages: a fraction of a year, against events {decay.minAge} to {decay.maxAge} years old. The
        {' '}{decay.spanDays} days since the register was founded now come to {decimal(decay.ageShiftYears, 2)} years,
        so that sentence no longer describes the archive, and the decision not to build the fit stands only on the
        reasons below until someone reads it again against the record.
      </p>
    )}
    <p class="mt-3 text-fg-muted">
      <strong>And the news is what a fit would read.</strong> {ofEvents(decay.belowFoundingRate, 'was', 'were')} invoked
      less per day since founding than in the window that chose them — the selection effect the register named when
      it was founded, now measured. {ofEvents(decay.mostlySilent, 'went', 'went')} unmentioned on most of the
      {' '}{decay.observedDays} days since.
      {decay.burst && (
        <>
          {' '}The busiest day any of them had was a burst: {count(decay.burst.mentions)} mentions of
          {' '}{humanDate(decay.burst.event)} on {humanDate(decay.burst.date)}, a date unmentioned on
          {' '}{decay.burst.silentDays} of those {decay.burst.observedDays} days.
        </>
      )}
      {' '}A curve fitted to this would turn anniversaries, news cycles and that selection effect into decay
      parameters: a number, not a test.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-fg-muted">
      <strong>One day's age profile cannot stand in.</strong> Fitting the decay to a single day's histogram of ages
      was rejected when the instrument was designed, and it stays rejected: that curve mixes how many datable events
      each year produced, and how large they were, with how fast they fade — several unknowns in one line.
      {decay.ceiling !== null && decay.youngestInvocableAge !== null && (
        <>
          {' '}And the source emits no year after {decay.ceiling}, so no age under {decay.youngestInvocableAge} years
          reaches this instrument: the young ages, where the fast phase of Candia's curve lives, are exactly the ones
          missing.
        </>
      )}
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      So the record says <code class="text-xs">not_built</code>, and says why, every night. The register is still
      counted — the table below — because the counts are the honest record of what happened to these dates. What
      they are not is a decay curve.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-line pl-5">
      <p class="kicker">What tonight's record says</p>
      {law.kind === 'not_built' && (
        <>
          <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm">not_built{law.since ? ` · since ${law.since}` : ''}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">In the pipeline's own words: {law.reason}.</p>
        </>
      )}
      {law.kind === 'withdrawn' && (
        <>
          <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm">pending</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">
            This record was written before the promise was withdrawn, and it still reads
            {' '}<code class="text-xs">pending</code>{law.firstPossible ? `, with a first possible fit on ${law.firstPossible}. That date is not a plan` : '. That state is not a plan'}:
            {' '}no fit followed it, none is planned, and records from the next run on carry
            {' '}<code class="text-xs">not_built</code> with their own reason.
          </p>
        </>
      )}
      {law.kind === 'other' && (
        <>
          <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-sm">{law.status}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">{law.reason}.</p>
        </>
      )}
    </div>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
      <p class="font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-faint">
        The register, counted: founded {register.founded} from {register.founding_window[0]} – {register.founding_window[1]};
        observed since on {decay.observedDays} measured days, the founding day excluded by its own selection effect.
      </p>
      <table class="mt-2 w-full border-collapse text-sm tabular-nums">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-line text-left kicker">
            <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium">Event date</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-right font-medium">age</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-right font-medium" title="mentions per day across the founding window that selected it">per day, founding</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-right font-medium" title="mentions per day across the measured days since founding">per day, since</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-3 text-right font-medium" title="days since founding on which it was not mentioned at all">days unmentioned</th>
            <th class="py-2 text-right font-medium">busiest day since</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {decay.events.map((e) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-line/50">
              <td class="py-1.5 pr-3">{humanDate(e.date)}</td>
              <td class="whitespace-nowrap py-1.5 pr-3 text-right font-mono text-xs">{count(e.age)} y</td>
              <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 text-right font-mono text-xs">{decimal(e.foundingPerDay, 1)}</td>
              <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 text-right font-mono text-xs">{e.sincePerDay === null ? '—' : decimal(e.sincePerDay, 1)}</td>
              <td class="py-1.5 pr-3 text-right font-mono text-xs">{e.silentDays} of {e.observedDays}</td>
              <td class="py-1.5 text-right font-mono text-xs">{e.busiest ? `${e.busiest.date} (${count(e.busiest.mentions)})` : '—'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="mt-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-fg-faint">
        Read from <code>src/data/invoked/register.json</code> and the day files since founding: a rate is mentions divided
        by days, nothing smoothed, and a day whose record does not carry an event is not counted as a zero for it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-12">
    <h2 class="kicker">What this measures — and what it does not</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <strong>The extraction is inherited, and its error modes with it.</strong> The instrument does not read
        text: it reads GDELT's own date field, {d.method.field}. Whatever that extractor mistakes for a date,
        this instrument counts as one. Four versioned cleaning rules run on top of it, and each reports what it
        removed, every night, in the day file — but no cleaning rule can repair a sensor from outside it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A year is not an event.</strong> 1989 carries Tiananmen and the fall of the Wall in the same
        bar, and any busy year is a container of several. Year-resolution mentions are what this field
        supplies in quantity, so the instrument reports invoked <em>years</em>, publishes what the articles
        invoking the standout year were about — and still leaves the naming of the event to whoever reads
        it. The register of tracked events holds dates, not names, chosen on {register.founded} by the
        archive's first {decay.foundingDays} days rather than by a single day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lift is a statement about today, not a verdict on the year.</strong> A theme appears because
        it is more common among the invoking articles than across the same day's articles; the comparison,
        the three floors and the raw codes are all published, so the ranking can be recomputed from the day
        file and disagreed with. The theme, person and organisation extraction is GDELT's own and inherits
        the looseness of the rest of the field — a phrase that is neither a person nor an organisation
        reaches this page unedited rather than being quietly dropped.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One country can carry a year on its own.</strong> That is why the standout is scored as an
        excess over its neighbourhood in units of that baseline's square root, and not as a plain ratio: on the
        first day of measurement the plain ratio preferred 1810, 65 of whose 73 mentions came from a single
        country. A Poisson-style surprise cannot be won by a thin neighbourhood alone, and the winner's country
        share is published beside it so a reader can discount it without being told to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The {ceiling} wall is the source's, and it is not papered over.</strong> No year later
        than {ceiling} is emitted at all, so the youngest age bucket is structurally empty and the ramp runs
        toward the ceiling year. That ramp carried the raw maximum to the wall on {series.maximumAtCeiling} of the
        {' '}{series.measured} measured days in the archive — {maxAtCeiling
          ? 'tonight included'
          : `not tonight, when ${d.most_invoked?.year} leads instead`}. Which of the two holds is read
        off the file each night and said plainly, never assumed; the missing stretch is drawn into the figure
        rather than cropped out of it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The English-monitored stream only.</strong> Pasts invoked mainly in other languages are
        underrepresented — a country that observes an anniversary loudly in its own language and quietly in
        English shows up here as the second thing. The source-country split additionally inherits GDELT's own
        domain-to-country attribution, which is demonstrably loose. The translation stream is the named v2
        upgrade.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The v1 stoplist is empty on purpose.</strong> A false-positive review of the day before launch
        found no systematic offender worth a blanket rule — so nothing is quietly removed. An empty list that is
        published is a measurement; an unpublished one is a habit.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-12">
    <h2 class="kicker">Nearest neighbors</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <a href="https://blog.gdeltproject.org/gdelt-2-0-our-global-world-in-realtime/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">GDELT's own <code class="text-xs">V2.1ENHANCEDDATES</code></a>
        — the sensor, added in 2015 explicitly for anniversary analysis. It has been emitting ever since; the
        legacy Analysis Service's anniversary visualiser that once read it is retired, and no living instrument
        took its place.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-018-0474-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">Candia et&nbsp;al. 2019, “The universal decay of collective memory and attention”</a>
        — Nature Human Behaviour. The decay law itself, on papers, patents, songs and films. A static paper, and the
        test hypothesis this instrument set out to run forward rather than its competitor — the decay section above
        says why its daily archive cannot.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.1199644" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">Michel et&nbsp;al. 2011, culturomics</a>
        — fame and its decay measured in Google Books. A historical corpus of what was printed, not a daily
        reading of what is being cited now.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:On_this_day" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-2 hover:text-fg">Wikipedia's “On this day”</a>
        — which anniversaries an editorial community decides to mark today. Curation, and a fine one; the
        opposite of a measurement of what the press actually invoked.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      The daylight: no living instrument of the press's invoked past exists. The nearest neighbours are a 2019
      paper and a retired visualiser — a sensor that has run for a decade with nothing reading it daily, no
      archive of what it saw, and no published method for calling one year a finding and another the ceiling.
    </p>
  </section>

